[description]

[b]Bitbucket Pipelines[/b] runs containerized steps declared in YAML at the repository root. Each step picks a Docker image, runs script sections, and can declare caches, artifacts, and deployment steps. Shared infrastructure enforces minute quotas—optimize with dependency caching and parallel steps where supported.

[b]Services:[/b] auxiliary containers (databases, emulators) can run beside the main step container for integration tests—similar in spirit to GitHub Actions service containers.

[b]Deployments:[/b] map steps to environments with manual gates for production; track release history alongside Jira versions when integrated.

[b]Secrets:[/b] repository and deployment variables should be scoped narrowly; rotation requires coordination with anyone embedding tokens in YAML (prefer injected vars over literals).

[b]Alternatives:[/b] many teams attach external CI (Bamboo, Circle, Jenkins) via webhooks while keeping PRs in Bitbucket—document the source of truth for green checks.

[b]Takeaway:[/b] treat pipeline YAML like application code: review changes, pin images, and test forks from untrusted contributors in isolated workspaces.
